225
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:36,560
 Early edentulous, complete upper and lower dentures and oh this is interesting reveals

226
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:42,200
 a radiopaque mask consistent with scialo lithiasis.

227
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:48,160
 So older patient sounds like maybe she has Shogren syndrome or maybe it's a side effect

228
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:50,080
 of polypharmacy.

229
00:18:50,080 --> 00:18:52,880
 We don't know why she has a dry mouth and frankly we don't care.

230
00:18:52,880 --> 00:18:54,440
 We don't need to know.

231
00:18:54,440 --> 00:18:58,200
 But what's important is this, the scialo lithiasis.

232
00:18:58,200 --> 00:19:05,880
 It's a calcification in a salivary gland duct and the submandibular gland has the highest

233
00:19:05,880 --> 00:19:12,480
 predilection for scialo lithiasis with about 80% occurrence rate followed by the prided

234
00:19:12,480 --> 00:19:19,560
 gland at about 19% and then sublingual glands for the remaining 1%.

235
00:19:19,560 --> 00:19:25,600
 But the question doesn't include the salivary glands as answer choices instead it's talking

236
00:19:25,600 --> 00:19:31,200
 about the salivary ducts which is where these calcifications tend to occur.

237
00:19:31,200 --> 00:19:36,600
 So we have to find the one that correlates to the submandibular gland.

238
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:42,120
 So if we go through these answer choices one at a time, the Stenson's duct has to do with

239
00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:45,840
 the prided gland so that is not correct.

240
00:19:45,840 --> 00:19:51,600
 Bartholins duct is part of the ducts of Rivenis which have to do with the sublingual gland

241
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:58,040
 that's also incorrect and the von Eppner's glands are glands found in a trough circling

242
00:19:58,040 --> 00:20:04,800
 the circumvalleyed papillae on the dorsal surface of the tongue near the terminal sulcus.

243
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:10,360
 And although it has to do with the tongue and taste buds although the bad taste might

244
00:20:10,360 --> 00:20:12,840
 tempt you to pick this answer choice.

245
00:20:12,840 --> 00:20:18,040
 The source of that bad taste is the scialo lith and this has nothing to do with calcifications

246
00:20:18,040 --> 00:20:20,180
 in a salivary gland duct.

247
00:20:20,180 --> 00:20:23,480
 So we're going to rule that one out as well.

248
00:20:23,480 --> 00:20:32,240
 Wartin's duct does connect to the submandibular gland and so that is the correct answer.

249
00:20:32,240 --> 00:20:41,400
 The submandibular gland is also responsible for producing the majority of our saliva.

250
00:20:41,400 --> 00:20:47,720
 Alright question number 11, which skull suture do you expect has fused early in this patient?

251
00:20:47,720 --> 00:20:52,520
 Go ahead pause the video and then we'll go over this together.

252
00:20:52,520 --> 00:20:54,920
 So here we have an 8 year old male.

253
00:20:54,920 --> 00:20:59,920
 His parent says, "My child has an underbite and I'm concerned about his adult teeth not

254
00:20:59,920 --> 00:21:01,440
 coming in.

255
00:21:01,440 --> 00:21:06,640
 And current findings include frontal bossing of the skull, hypertealarism, increased distance

256
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:15,000
 between the eyes, proptosis which is bulging eyes, and a hypoplastic or a retrusive maxilla.

257
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:20,760
 They're currently in primary dentition with multiple impacted permanent teeth."

258
00:21:20,760 --> 00:21:27,640
 So really the first part of solving this puzzle is figuring out this child's diagnosis.

259
00:21:27,640 --> 00:21:34,160
 And all these findings together point towards a syndrome being cruzon syndrome.

260
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:39,880
 Now the second half of this puzzle is determining the suture involved in early fusion.

261
00:21:39,880 --> 00:21:46,800
 Now cruzon's patients present with brachycephaly, that's a short skull antaroposterially, which

262
00:21:46,800 --> 00:21:50,240
 we talked about when we talked about skull sutures.

263
00:21:50,240 --> 00:21:56,440
 So it makes sense that a suture that runs horizontally would most likely be involved

264
00:21:56,440 --> 00:22:04,800
 in a craniosynostosis that results in a short AP or antaroposterior skull.

265
00:22:04,800 --> 00:22:13,000
 So the only one of these that runs horizontally is the coronal suture.

266
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:19,000
 And the coronal suture is in fact the one that tends to be fused early in these cruzon and

267
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,600
 apert patients.

268
00:22:21,600 --> 00:22:27,680
 And so we can see here again from our drawing that the frontal or metopic, the sagittal and

269
00:22:27,680 --> 00:22:34,960
 the squamous sutures all run from this view a vertical direction, and the coronal is the

270
00:22:34,960 --> 00:22:39,600
 only one that runs in a horizontal direction, which is going to prohibit the skull from

271
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:42,320
 expanding along that axis.

272
00:22:42,320 --> 00:22:46,760
 So the coronal suture is the correct answer for this question.

273
00:22:46,760 --> 00:22:53,320
 All right, question number 12, which of the following structures does not border the infected

274
00:22:53,320 --> 00:23:01,360
 facial space, go ahead pause the video and then we'll talk about this one together.

275
00:23:01,360 --> 00:23:04,000
 So here we have a 25 year old male.

276
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:07,360
 His face is swollen and he cannot open his mouth.

277
00:23:07,360 --> 00:23:09,400
 This is called trismus.

278
00:23:09,400 --> 00:23:14,640
 And his previous dentist inadvertently used a contaminated needle and infected a facial

279
00:23:14,640 --> 00:23:19,240
 space during an inferior alveolar nerve block.

280
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:20,920
 So this is interesting.

281
00:23:20,920 --> 00:23:25,880
 That has resulted in trismus and lateral facial swelling.

282
00:23:25,880 --> 00:23:30,460
 And so which of the following structures does not border the infected facial space?

283
00:23:30,460 --> 00:23:35,840
 Well, what we have to first know is which facial space is infected.

284
00:23:35,840 --> 00:23:40,960
 And the one that's typically involved with an inferior alveolar nerve block is the Terego

285
00:23:40,960 --> 00:23:42,920
 mandibular space.

286
00:23:42,920 --> 00:23:50,880
 If we draw our diamond, this is my shortcut for the masticator spaces.

287
00:23:50,880 --> 00:23:53,200
 And so we have the zygomatic arch here.

288
00:23:53,200 --> 00:23:56,440
 We have the mandible that goes down here.

289
00:23:56,440 --> 00:24:02,400
 We have the skull right up here and we have a couple of muscles of mastication.

290
00:24:02,400 --> 00:24:08,720
 We have the mastider muscle, the medial pterogoid, the lateral pterogoid and the temporalis.

291
00:24:08,720 --> 00:24:14,280
 And so the space that we're really interested in is, again, the Terego mandibular space,

292
00:24:14,280 --> 00:24:21,360
 which is located between the lateral pterogoid muscle and the mandible.

293
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:25,720
 And so which of the following structures does not border that infected facial space?

294
00:24:25,720 --> 00:24:29,600
 Well, we just talked about we have the mandibular ramus.

295
00:24:29,600 --> 00:24:35,080
 We have the lateral pterogoid muscle and we have the medial pterogoid muscle.

296
00:24:35,080 --> 00:24:40,800
 And so we can rule out medial pterogoid, we can rule out lateral pterogoid.

297
00:24:40,800 --> 00:24:46,440
 Now, the interesting thing is we said mandibular ramus because that's the vertical part of

298
00:24:46,440 --> 00:24:47,680
 the mandible.

299
00:24:47,680 --> 00:24:54,240
 But the mandibular body is located too far down and it's not involved in this facial

300
00:24:54,240 --> 00:24:55,240
 space.

301
00:24:55,240 --> 00:24:59,000
 You can think back where we're doing an inferior alveolar nerve block.

302
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,560
 The body is located too far forward and down.

303
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:07,120
 For comprehensiveness sake, let's talk about every single boundary of this Terego mandibular

304
00:25:07,120 --> 00:25:08,280
 space.

305
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:13,200
 And superiorly, we have the inferior head of the lateral pterogoid, laterally, we have

306
00:25:13,200 --> 00:25:20,720
 the medial surface of the mandibular ramus, medially, we have the medial pterogoid muscle,

307
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:26,720
 posteriorly, we have the deep part of the parotid gland and anteriorly, we have the Terego

308
00:25:26,720 --> 00:25:28,920
 mandibular refe.

309
00:25:28,920 --> 00:25:36,840
 And so the parotid gland is in fact a deep or posterior boundary for this space.

310
00:25:36,840 --> 00:25:41,720
 The mandibular body is not, it's actually the mandibular ramus.

311
00:25:41,720 --> 00:25:46,280
 So pretty challenging question here, but the correct answer for which of the following

312
00:25:46,280 --> 00:25:51,160
 does not border the Terego mandibular space is the mandibular body.

313
00:25:51,160 --> 00:25:54,680
 And here's that diagram that I was drawing before.

314
00:25:54,680 --> 00:26:03,200
 All right, question number 13, now the following three questions all have to do with the same

315
00:26:03,200 --> 00:26:04,200
 case.

316
00:26:04,200 --> 00:26:09,320
 So this is a classic example of a case set where you're given one patient, then several

317
00:26:09,320 --> 00:26:12,760
 questions concerning that single patient.

318
00:26:12,760 --> 00:26:16,480
 So you decide to conduct a cranial nerve exam for this patient.

319
00:26:16,480 --> 00:26:21,720
 First you ask her to follow your finger with her eyes, making sure to test for full range

320
00:26:21,720 --> 00:26:27,540
 of motion of each eye, which of the following cranial nerves have you not tested?

321
00:26:27,540 --> 00:26:34,600
 So go ahead, read through this question, the patient box, and then we'll go over it together.

322
00:26:34,600 --> 00:26:41,200
 Okay, so here we have a 60 year old female, she hasn't been to the dentist in a while,

323
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:43,280
 and she has a history of Bell's palsy.

324
00:26:43,280 --> 00:26:48,240
 So that's facial paralysis on one side of the face.

325
00:26:48,240 --> 00:26:53,520
 So a cranial nerve exam might be a good idea to test her range of motion and see what's

326
00:26:53,520 --> 00:26:55,680
 going on.

327
00:26:55,680 --> 00:27:01,480
 So for this first part of the cranial nerve exam, we're asking her to follow our finger.

328
00:27:01,480 --> 00:27:06,560
 So she's moving the pupils of her eyes up and down left and right, and we're testing

329
00:27:06,560 --> 00:27:09,880
 for a complete full range of motion.

330
00:27:09,880 --> 00:27:19,200
 So the cranial nerves here, three, oculomotor, four, and six, are all controlling extraocular

331
00:27:19,200 --> 00:27:20,920
 eye muscles.

332
00:27:20,920 --> 00:27:29,920
 Three is controlling most of those muscles, and then we have our SO4LR6, so superior oblique

333
00:27:29,920 --> 00:27:32,520
 and lateral rectus.

334
00:27:32,520 --> 00:27:38,320
 Now the trigeminal nerve not involved in this, it's involved in sensation of the face, we

335
00:27:38,320 --> 00:27:41,120
 have some tongue innervation going on.

336
00:27:41,120 --> 00:27:47,520
 So this is not involving the activity of the eye.

337
00:27:47,520 --> 00:27:54,280
 So the answer for this first question of the case set is C. Now I might even make the argument

338
00:27:54,280 --> 00:28:00,080
 that this test of testing their full range of motion of the eye really doesn't test the

339
00:28:00,080 --> 00:28:03,680
 cranial nerve to either, that's the optic nerve.

340
00:28:03,680 --> 00:28:08,080
 That would be done with a field of view test and a visual acuity test like you'd have

341
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:13,920
 at the eye doctor where you have to read certain letters from a certain distance.

342
00:28:13,920 --> 00:28:19,440
 And so my point for bringing that up is that if one of the answer choices was cranial nerve

343
00:28:19,440 --> 00:28:26,360
 2, I probably would select that as the correct answer as well because this specific test

344
00:28:26,360 --> 00:28:34,760
 is not really testing the activity of cranial nerve 2 specifically, it's more focusing on

345
00:28:34,760 --> 00:28:37,720
 the actual muscle movements of the eye.

346
00:28:37,720 --> 00:28:43,280
 Can both eyes accurately move in both in all directions?

347
00:28:43,280 --> 00:28:49,280
 So the answer for this first one is C. Then the second question for the case is next you

348
00:28:49,280 --> 00:28:53,960
 ask the patient to say ah and observe movement at the soft palate.

349
00:28:53,960 --> 00:28:56,600
 Which of the following cranial nerves are you testing now?

350
00:28:56,600 --> 00:29:03,280
 Go ahead pause the video and then we'll go through this together.

351
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:10,280
 So motor movement of all soft palate muscles except the tensor of belly palatini is controlled

352
00:29:10,280 --> 00:29:12,600
 by the vagus nerve.

353
00:29:12,600 --> 00:29:18,280
 So the answer to this one is going to be cranial nerve 10, the vagus nerve.

354
00:29:18,280 --> 00:29:23,680
 Now if we go through the incorrect choices, the facial nerve does facial expressions which

355
00:29:23,680 --> 00:29:29,960
 are not relevant here, the glossopharyngeal nerve is responsible for the sensory gag reflex

356
00:29:29,960 --> 00:29:34,400
 but not motor movements of the soft palate when you're saying something like ah.

357
00:29:34,400 --> 00:29:36,200
 So that's also incorrect.

358
00:29:36,200 --> 00:29:41,360
 And the hypoglossal nerve does activate when you say ah if you stick your tongue out like

359
00:29:41,360 --> 00:29:46,300
 at the doctor's office but that has to do with tongue movement, not soft palate movement

360
00:29:46,300 --> 00:29:49,480
 which the question stem points out.

361
00:29:49,480 --> 00:29:55,360
 So we're looking for soft palate vibration which is activated by cranial nerve 10 or

362
00:29:55,360 --> 00:29:58,640
 the vagus nerve.

363
00:29:58,640 --> 00:30:02,440
 And finally you ask the patient to shrug their shoulders.

364
00:30:02,440 --> 00:30:06,200
 Which of the following cranial nerves are you testing now?

365
00:30:06,200 --> 00:30:12,880
 Go ahead pause the video and then we'll go through this together.

366
00:30:12,880 --> 00:30:19,000
 Alright so for this last question, the main muscle that shrugs the shoulder is the trapezius

367
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:24,720
 muscle and it's one of the two muscles that's innervated by the spinal accessory nerve which

368
00:30:24,720 --> 00:30:27,360
 is cranial nerve 11.

369
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:33,440
 So the answer for this final question of these practice questions is D.

370
00:30:33,440 --> 00:30:36,280
 Alright so how'd you do?

